---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TileCalculator from '../components/TileCalculator.astro';
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="How Many Tiles Do I Need? Free Calculator | HomeDIYCalcs"
  description="Use this free calculator to find out exactly how many tiles you need for any project. Just enter dimensions and tile size."
>
  <section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <div class="mb-12 text-center">
      <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl text-ink mb-4">
        How many tiles do I need?
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-ink-muted max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        The short answer: divide the total area by the size of one tile, then add 10% for waste. Use the calculator below for an exact number.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TileCalculator />

    <div class="mt-16 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">The simple formula</h2>
      <p class="text-ink mb-4">To calculate tiles by hand:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-ink space-y-2 mb-8">
        <li>Measure your area (length × width) to get total square feet.</li>
        <li>Calculate the size of one tile in square feet (e.g. 12×12 inch = 1 sq ft).</li>
        <li>Divide total area by tile area to get base tile count.</li>
        <li>Multiply by 1.10 for 10% waste, or 1.15 for diagonal/complex layouts.</li>
        <li>Round up to whole tiles or boxes.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Common tile counts at a glance</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-muted mb-4">
        For 12×12 inch tiles with 10% waste:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink-muted space-y-1 mb-8">
        <li>50 sq ft area: about 55 tiles</li>
        <li>100 sq ft area: about 110 tiles</li>
        <li>200 sq ft area: about 220 tiles</li>
        <li>500 sq ft area: about 550 tiles</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-ink mb-2">Why do I need extra tiles for waste?</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-muted">Tiles get cut around edges, walls, fixtures, and corners. Some tiles also break during cutting or have visible defects. The waste factor accounts for all of this.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-ink mb-2">What if my room isn't a perfect rectangle?</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-muted">Break the area into rectangles and calculate each one separately. Add the results together. For circular or unusual shapes, calculate the bounding rectangle and add 10% extra.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-ink mb-2">Should I buy extra for future repairs?</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-muted">Yes — buy 1–2 extra boxes beyond your calculation. Tile production runs change over years, and matching dye lots later is nearly impossible. Better to have a few spares stored.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>